WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:05.810
From slicing, we move to data structure, also
known as collection data types, which are

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.190
types of lists stored inside of variables.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:14.360
You have seen how indexing and slicing work
with items in a list, but we have yet to cover

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.629
how to build a list.

00:00:15.629 --> 00:00:18.230
We will do that in the next topic.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:24.650
This topic will look at three other data types:
tuples, sets, and dictionaries.

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:29.820
I want you to really focus on two pieces from
each type: what the structure does and the

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.750
syntax used to build each structure.

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:39.070
Strings themselves are data structures as
they are collections of characters.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:43.539
Dictionaries store data in key-value pairs.

00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:49.780
If, for example, you have a game where you
have a point value for each type of coin someone

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:56.980
earns based on answering a question, you could
set up a key-value pair list for those coins,

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.250
as we have on lines 1 through 6 of this 124-structure
file.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:07.700
Notice that the collection of key-value pairs
is inside curly brackets, and the syntax goes

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:11.800
key: value.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:18.500
In printing this dictionary, as the code on
line 7 does, we see the complete set of keys,

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.270
the coins, and their values.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:26.979
A set is a type of collection that stores
unique values of data.

00:01:26.979 --> 00:01:33.329
On line 10, I have started a list with three
regions, and the command to print the list

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:35.729
is on line 12.

00:01:35.729 --> 00:01:47.210
Watch what happens when I try to add a duplicate
value by typing regions.add(“east”) 

00:01:47.210 --> 00:01:51.060
on line 11 and then run the code.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:59.969
We don’t get any errors, which is good,
but East is not in the list twice because,

00:01:59.969 --> 00:02:04.850
as mentioned, each value in this type of list
is unique.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:12.890
However, if I change this value to add West
and then rerun the code, the value is added

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:18.900
as it is a unique value compared to the rest
of the existing values in the set.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:25.530
Lastly, we have tuples, which is a list with
immutable values.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:30.450
You may hear the word immutable often with
values in programming.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:34.390
Immutable means unchangeable, as the only
way to change what is in a variable is to

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:36.550
redeclare the variable.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:46.180
Case in point: I have a tuple, on line 15,
with a game character’s name and animal

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:47.790
type.

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:58.950
If I try to change the second value, index
1, to another animal 

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:07.280
and run this code, an error message indicates
that we cannot assign a new value to that

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:12.430
index because tuples are immutable, as we
just said.

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:16.780
Tuples are good for lists that should not
have their items changed.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:21.620
Knowing which type of data structure to use,
given a situation, makes apps perform more

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:26.700
smoothly, as some data structures are meant
to have unique values, protecting from duplicate

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:27.780
data.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:35.590
Some structures are intended to be immutable,
and some need to store data in key-value pairs.

